1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,840
Why do intelligent organizations and disciplined individuals repeatedly step

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:06,814
into traps of their own making?

3
00:00:06,814 --> 00:00:07,706
Almost always,

4
00:00:07,706 --> 00:00:10,508
the culprit is stopping at the first domino.

5
00:00:10,508 --> 00:00:13,693
First-order thinking looks at an annoying problem,

6
00:00:13,693 --> 00:00:15,603
grabs the fastest visible fix,

7
00:00:15,603 --> 00:00:17,641
and celebrates immediate relief.

8
00:00:17,641 --> 00:00:21,399
Answering messages all morning clears your inbox right now,

9
00:00:21,399 --> 00:00:24,392
yet trains colleagues to interrupt you forever.

10
00:00:24,392 --> 00:00:27,385
Financing a luxury car lowers today's friction,

11
00:00:27,385 --> 00:00:30,315
yet locks up five years of investable surplus.

12
00:00:30,315 --> 00:00:34,709
Second-order thinking pauses before acting and asks four quiet words:

13
00:00:34,709 --> 00:00:36,110
and then what happens?

14
00:00:36,110 --> 00:00:36,629
In 1850,

15
00:00:36,629 --> 00:00:40,779
French economist Frederic Bastiat wrote a brilliant essay titled

16
00:00:40,779 --> 00:00:42,012
That Which Is Seen,

17
00:00:42,012 --> 00:00:43,763
and That Which Is Not Seen.

18
00:00:43,763 --> 00:00:44,606
Every choice,

19
00:00:44,606 --> 00:00:45,579
Bastiat argued,

20
00:00:45,579 --> 00:00:47,459
produces two sets of effects:

21
00:00:47,459 --> 00:00:49,340
an immediate visible outcome,

22
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:50,832
and a chain of delayed,

23
00:00:50,832 --> 00:00:54,593
unseen consequences that unfold as human incentives adapt.

24
00:00:54,593 --> 00:00:58,614
Economist Horst Siebert later illustrated this with the famous

25
00:00:58,614 --> 00:01:00,754
Cobra Effect from colonial Delhi:

26
00:01:00,754 --> 00:01:04,127
officials offered a cash bounty for every dead cobra

27
00:01:04,127 --> 00:01:06,137
to shrink the snake population.

28
00:01:06,137 --> 00:01:07,369
First-order result:

29
00:01:07,369 --> 00:01:08,731
piles of dead snakes.

30
00:01:08,731 --> 00:01:10,288
Second-order adaptation:

31
00:01:10,288 --> 00:01:15,087
enterprising citizens started breeding cobras at home to collect bounties,

32
00:01:15,087 --> 00:01:18,070
leaving the city with more snakes than before.

33
00:01:18,070 --> 00:01:22,632
Oaktree Capital co-founder Howard Marks placed this exact discipline

34
00:01:22,632 --> 00:01:25,316
in chapter one of his 2011 book The Most

35
00:01:25,316 --> 00:01:26,389
Important Thing.

36
00:01:26,389 --> 00:01:29,811
Marks points out that first-level thinking is fast,

37
00:01:29,811 --> 00:01:30,616
superficial,

38
00:01:30,616 --> 00:01:33,099
and shared by everyone in the market.

39
00:01:33,099 --> 00:01:34,843
First-level thinking says:

40
00:01:34,843 --> 00:01:36,789
this company is growing fast,

41
00:01:36,789 --> 00:01:38,265
let us buy the shares.

42
00:01:38,265 --> 00:01:40,076
Second-level thinking says:

43
00:01:40,076 --> 00:01:42,022
this company is growing fast,

44
00:01:42,022 --> 00:01:45,108
every analyst on Wall Street already knows it,

45
00:01:45,108 --> 00:01:48,396
and expectations are priced so high that even one

46
00:01:48,396 --> 00:01:50,744
slight hiccup will crush the stock.

47
00:01:50,744 --> 00:01:53,494
In investing and business strategy alike,

48
00:01:53,494 --> 00:01:57,520
doing what feels obvious guarantees at best average results.

49
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:01,806
Second-order thinking also works in reverse when evaluating habits

50
00:02:01,806 --> 00:02:03,624
and budgets you already own.

51
00:02:03,624 --> 00:02:04,144
In 1929,

52
00:02:04,144 --> 00:02:04,728
writer G.

53
00:02:04,728 --> 00:02:04,988
K.

54
00:02:04,988 --> 00:02:08,820
Chesterton warned reformers never to tear down a mysterious

55
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:11,937
fence across a road until they could explain why

56
00:02:11,937 --> 00:02:14,210
previous builders paid to erect it.

57
00:02:14,210 --> 00:02:17,912
Many personal and corporate guardrails look wasteful to a

58
00:02:17,912 --> 00:02:18,951
first-order eye:

59
00:02:18,951 --> 00:02:21,159
six months of idle emergency cash,

60
00:02:21,159 --> 00:02:23,042
an eight-hour sleep boundary,

61
00:02:23,042 --> 00:02:24,795
a pre-deployment checklist,

62
00:02:24,795 --> 00:02:29,016
or refusing to take on client concentration above thirty percent.

63
00:02:29,016 --> 00:02:32,653
Each one carries a small visible cost today specifically

64
00:02:32,653 --> 00:02:36,160
to block a catastrophic second-order failure tomorrow.

65
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:40,196
Notice the striking mathematical pattern across almost every major

66
00:02:40,196 --> 00:02:40,930
life domain:

67
00:02:40,930 --> 00:02:43,804
the sign flips between order one and order two.

68
00:02:43,804 --> 00:02:44,416
Junk food,

69
00:02:44,416 --> 00:02:45,272
consumer debt,

70
00:02:45,272 --> 00:02:46,128
doomscrolling,

71
00:02:46,128 --> 00:02:50,531
and avoiding hard conversations all deliver positive first-order comfort

72
00:02:50,531 --> 00:02:52,060
in the first ten minutes,

73
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:56,158
followed by negative second-order interest over the next ten years.

74
00:02:56,158 --> 00:02:56,830
Conversely,

75
00:02:56,830 --> 00:02:58,420
automated index investing,

76
00:02:58,420 --> 00:02:59,521
strength training,

77
00:02:59,521 --> 00:03:00,622
deep solo writing,

78
00:03:00,622 --> 00:03:04,964
and candid feedback all carry mildly uncomfortable first-order friction

79
00:03:04,964 --> 00:03:05,514
up front,

80
00:03:05,514 --> 00:03:08,633
followed by extraordinary second-order compounding.

81
00:03:08,633 --> 00:03:11,630
Whenever a daily habit feels slightly hard in the

82
00:03:11,630 --> 00:03:12,731
first ten minutes,

83
00:03:12,731 --> 00:03:16,400
you are usually standing in front of an uncrowded advantage.

84
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:18,828
Before signing any major contract,

85
00:03:18,828 --> 00:03:20,542
making a large purchase,

86
00:03:20,542 --> 00:03:22,470
or deleting an old routine,

87
00:03:22,470 --> 00:03:25,398
run three written prompts on half a page.

88
00:03:25,398 --> 00:03:29,897
Prompt one is the Ten-Ten-Ten Horizon popularized by journalist

89
00:03:29,897 --> 00:03:30,682
Suzy Welch:

90
00:03:30,682 --> 00:03:34,181
write down how this choice will feel in ten days,

91
00:03:34,181 --> 00:03:34,967
ten months,

92
00:03:34,967 --> 00:03:35,966
and ten years.

93
00:03:35,966 --> 00:03:38,823
Prompt two is Bastiat's Incentive Check:

94
00:03:38,823 --> 00:03:40,680
if this works at step one,

95
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:42,893
how will your monthly overhead,

96
00:03:42,893 --> 00:03:43,822
your clients,

97
00:03:43,822 --> 00:03:46,535
or your competitors adapt at step two?

98
00:03:46,535 --> 00:03:49,035
Prompt three is Chesterton's Audit:

99
00:03:49,035 --> 00:03:53,034
never remove a financial or operational buffer until you

100
00:03:53,034 --> 00:03:55,890
can state the exact failure it prevents.
